column2:
You returned a pointer to a temporary. It's nasty but easy to spot.<p>Would you return `input.c_str()`? You might think twice about that, but it's the same thing. My favorite fix is to take a `std::string_view` as the parameter.<p><pre><code>    string_view remove_leading_spaces(string_view input) {
        auto new_start = find_if(input.begin(), input.end(), [](char ch) { return ch != ' '; });
        return string_view(new_start, input.end());
    }
</code></pre>
The result of the undefined behavior in your program is surprising, though. As it usually is.
